## PART 2: TECHNICAL

### Q7. Walk me through how you'd approach designing a cloud-ready, scalable data service from scratch.

**A:** I'd start with the data contract and access patterns before infrastructure — who consumes this data, at what latency, and at what volume — because that drives whether you need event-driven vs. request/response, and what your consistency requirements actually are. This is basically what we did at IBM: we moved to an event-driven lineage architecture specifically because downstream consumers needed near-real-time traceability, not because event-driven is inherently "better."

From there: define service boundaries using standard microservices patterns, expose them via REST or GraphQL depending on consumer needs (we used GraphQL at IBM for flexible, insight-heavy queries against ingested data, and plain REST for the Watson Search recommender service where the access pattern was simpler), and build in observability (telemetry, SLAs, anomaly detection — this is core to how we run things at JPMorgan) from day one rather than bolting it on later. For cloud-readiness specifically, I design for statelessness where possible and externalize configuration and secrets (we use AWS Parameter Store at OpenText), so the service can scale horizontally without surprises.

### Q8. Tell me about a large-scale data integration project you led — what made it complex, and how did you handle system integration design patterns?

**A:** *(Situation)* At IBM, I owned the data governance and eDiscovery platform (Watson Knowledge Catalog), which had to ingest, process, and expose lineage information across many downstream consumer services with very different needs.

*(Task)* Data consistency across those downstream services was poor — reported around 70% before we intervened — largely because each consumer had built its own point-to-point integration with upstream sources.

*(Action)* I redesigned this as an event-driven integration layer — effectively a data-fabric pattern — where upstream changes were published as events and consumers subscribed to what they needed, rather than each building custom pulls. I also introduced Go-based concurrency in the ingestion phase specifically to handle the throughput this generated without becoming a bottleneck.

*(Result)* Data consistency and traceability improved by 70% across downstream services, and the failure ratio in ingestion dropped 40% due to the concurrency work. It also meant onboarding a new downstream consumer became a matter of subscribing to existing events rather than a multi-week custom integration project — which is really the point of a data-fabric approach.

### Q9. What's your experience with messaging and streaming technologies, and how have you used them in production?

**A:** Kafka has been core to most of my recent architecture work. At IBM we used Kafka Messaging as the backbone of our event-driven lineage architecture — publishing data-change events that downstream services (including the Watson Search recommender, which combined this with Cloudant DB and the ELK stack) consumed asynchronously. This decoupled producers from consumers, which was critical given how many different downstream teams we supported with very different SLAs.

More broadly, I think about streaming architectures in terms of failure modes — what happens on consumer lag, on duplicate delivery, on ordering guarantees you don't actually have — and I've had to design around all three of those at various points, which is a big part of why the ingestion layer needed the concurrency and resiliency work.

### Q10. How do you approach REST API and microservices design for maintainability and scale?

**A:** I lean on a few consistent design patterns rather than reinventing per-service: clear service boundaries around a single responsibility, contract-first API design so consumers aren't surprised by changes, and resiliency patterns (circuit breakers, retries with backoff) baked in rather than assumed. At Pramati, I specifically worked on request-driven microservices with resiliency patterns for data procurement — that's really where I internalized that resiliency has to be designed in, not added after an incident.

For maintainability specifically, I care a lot about consistent patterns across a codebase — at ADP, my entire engagement was retrofitting design patterns into an existing system specifically to make it scalable and extensible enough to become a platform, which taught me that inconsistent patterns are often a bigger long-term cost than any individual architectural choice.

### Q11. Describe your experience with DevOps, CI/CD, and engineering best practices — what does "good" look like to you?

**A:** "Good" CI/CD to me means a change can go from commit to production with high confidence and minimal manual intervention, and when something does go wrong, you find out from your own telemetry before your customer tells you. At OpenText, I built out CI/CD integration using GitHub and Jenkins into AWS as part of the roadmap for our SaaS platform, and at IBM we specifically restructured our pipelines and Agile/Scrum cadence to cut deployment cycles by 3x.

I also think good DevOps culture is a leadership responsibility, not just a tooling one — teams cut corners on testing or observability when they're under delivery pressure unless a manager explicitly protects that time. Part of my role is making sure "we'll add tests later" doesn't become permanent.

### Q12. Tell me about a production incident you handled, and how you approached root cause and prevention.

**A:** *(Situation)* On the IBM on-premises SOA-based product, we periodically hit critical customer-blocking issues that required back-porting fixes across product versions — always high-pressure, since customers were blocked.

*(Task)* Beyond just fixing the immediate issue, I wanted to reduce how often these blockers happened in the first place.

*(Action)* I pushed for the model monitoring and feedback systems we eventually built — instrumenting the product itself to surface issues from telemetry before customers reported them, rather than purely reactive support. This connects directly to what I do now at JPMorgan, where we've built proactive anomaly detection and SLA-based observability specifically so incidents are caught upstream.

*(Result)* Post-deployment customer satisfaction improved 25% and support requests dropped 60% — a meaningful chunk of that was simply catching issues before they became customer-visible blockers.

