## Section 1: Leadership & People Management (15)

**1. How would you describe your leadership style?**
I lead with clarity and trust — setting a clear technical direction, then giving engineers real ownership over how they get there. At JPMorgan and OpenText, I found the best outcomes came from pairing high autonomy with tight alignment on outcomes, not prescribing implementation details. I adjust based on the engineer's experience level: more coaching for junior folks, more removing-roadblocks for senior ones.

**2. Tell me about a time you managed an underperforming engineer.**
I start by diagnosing whether it's a skill gap, a motivation issue, or unclear expectations — they require very different responses. I've had cases at IBM where an engineer was struggling with unclear scope rather than raw capability; setting explicit weekly goals and pairing them with a senior engineer turned that around within a quarter. If performance doesn't improve after clear feedback and support, I move to a formal plan rather than letting it drag on, since that's unfair to the whole team.

**3. How do you build trust with a new team?**
I listen before I change anything — first few weeks are about understanding how the team actually works, what's broken, and who the informal leaders are. When I stepped into the AI Platforms org at JPMorgan, I ran 1:1s with every engineer before proposing the chain-of-agents architecture, so the design incorporated their pain points rather than being handed down.

**4. How do you handle conflict between two senior engineers on your team?**
I get both sides separately first, then bring them together focused on the technical decision, not personalities. Usually disagreements are really about unstated tradeoffs — I try to make those tradeoffs explicit (latency vs. cost, flexibility vs. simplicity) and let data or a quick prototype settle it rather than my opinion alone.

**5. Describe your approach to giving difficult feedback.**
Direct, timely, and private. I don't let issues accumulate for a performance review — I raise them close to the moment, focused on specific behavior and impact rather than character. I also make sure the person leaves the conversation knowing exactly what "better" looks like.

**6. How do you motivate a team during a high-pressure delivery period?**
Transparency about the "why" and protecting the team from noise above them. When we were delivering the Agentic AI platform at JPMorgan against a business-driven launch date, I made sure the team understood how the work mapped to campaign teams' real pain points, and I absorbed a lot of the stakeholder pressure myself so they could focus.

**7. How do you decide when to make a call yourself vs. build team consensus?**
Reversible, low-blast-radius decisions I push down to the team or make quickly myself. Decisions that are expensive to reverse — architecture choices like the Intent/Orchestrator agent split, or platform-wide standards — I involve the senior engineers, because the diversity of input reduces the risk of a costly mistake.

**8. Tell me about a time you had to deliver bad news to your team.**
At OpenText, when roadmap priorities shifted mid-quarter, I told the team plainly what changed and why, rather than softening it or letting it filter through rumor. People handle hard truths better than uncertainty.

**9. How do you manage a team that spans multiple time zones or is distributed?**
Bias toward async-first documentation so no single overlap-window meeting is a single point of failure, then use the overlap time we do have for genuine discussion rather than status updates. Across IBM and JPMorgan, most of my teams involved cross-geo coordination with global engineering peers.

**10. How do you balance being hands-on technically with pure people management?**
I stay technical enough to review architecture and unblock hard problems, but I intentionally don't write production code day-to-day — my leverage as a manager is in decisions, structure, and unblocking, not lines of code. I do stay close enough to the code (e.g., in the agent orchestration design) to ask good questions and catch risk early.

**11. How do you onboard new engineers effectively?**
Structured ramp plan with a real (small) deliverable in week one, paired with a buddy, and explicit context on the "why" behind the architecture — not just the "how." At OpenText, growing the team to eight, I made sure new hires shipped something real within the first two weeks to build confidence and surface gaps quickly.

**12. Describe a time you had to let someone go.**
I treat it as a last resort after clear, documented feedback and real support have failed, and when it happens I do it with as much dignity and directness as possible — no ambiguity about why, and no surprises, because the conversation should never be the first time they're hearing the concern.

**13. How do you handle a high performer who wants to leave for another opportunity?**
I ask what's driving it honestly, and if it's addressable (scope, growth path, comp) I act fast — but I don't try to guilt or over-negotiate someone into staying if it's not genuinely the right move for them. Losing people well, with a good reference and door open, matters more long-term than any single retention.

**14. What's your approach to succession planning on your team?**
I actively delegate ownership of specific subsystems to senior engineers so there's always a next-in-line who understands the "why," not just the "what." At JPMorgan, the domain-specific service agent owners each had end-to-end ownership, which reduces bus-factor risk.

**15. How do you scale your management style as your team grows from 5 to 20+ people?**
Below ~7 direct reports, I manage everyone directly. Beyond that, I invest in tech leads/leads-of-leads and shift my own time toward architecture, cross-team alignment, and hiring — the job becomes less about individual coaching and more about designing the org so good decisions happen without me in the room.

