## Section 2: Engineering Strategy, Architecture & System Design (15)

**16. Walk me through the chain-of-agents architecture you built at JPMorgan.**
We split responsibilities into an Intent agent (interprets the prompt-driven campaign request), an Orchestrator agent (plans and sequences the workflow), and domain-specific service agents (execute discrete tasks against enterprise backends). This separation let us govern and audit each stage independently — important in a regulated bank — while keeping the system modular enough to add new domain agents without touching the core orchestration logic.

**17. How do you approach architecting a system for a regulated environment like banking?**
Security and auditability are first-class design constraints, not an afterthought layered on later. Every agent interaction in the JPMorgan platform is logged and governed, and I designed the workflow automation to keep a human-legible audit trail, because "the AI did it" is never an acceptable answer to a regulator.

**18. Tell me about a system you designed for scale. What were the key decisions?**
The IBM Watson Knowledge Catalog data lineage platform had to trace data across a huge, heterogeneous enterprise data estate. Key decisions were an event-driven ingestion pipeline (Kafka) to decouple producers from consumers, and a deduplication strategy that both cut infrastructure cost and improved traceability accuracy by 70%.

**19. How do you decide between build vs. buy for a platform capability?**
I weigh differentiation value against opportunity cost — if it's core to what makes our product defensible (like our agent orchestration logic), we build; if it's commodity infrastructure (observability tooling, CI/CD), we buy or use open source and put engineering time where it compounds.

**20. Describe your experience with on-device AI inference. Why does it matter?**
At JPMorgan I introduced on-device inference using ExecuTorch for parts of our AI workflow, which reduced latency by avoiding round-trips to a server, improved user privacy since sensitive data didn't need to leave the device, and helped with regulatory compliance around data residency — all three mattered simultaneously in a financial services context.

**21. How do you approach technical debt?**
I treat it like financial debt — some is a deliberate, tracked tradeoff to hit a deadline; unmanaged debt compounds and eventually stalls delivery. I keep a visible backlog of debt items with an owner and rough cost-of-delay, and I protect a percentage of each sprint for paying it down rather than letting it become a "someday" list.

**22. How do you evaluate whether to adopt a new technology (e.g., a new LLM framework)?**
I look for a real problem it solves better than our current stack, a low-risk way to pilot it (not a big-bang rewrite), and a realistic view of the maintenance burden it adds. When I brought Mistral LLM into the IBM compliance/explainability work, we piloted it on a narrow use case before expanding.

**23. How do you think about API and system design for extensibility?**
I aim for clear contracts and single-responsibility boundaries so a new capability is additive, not a change to existing code paths. The domain-specific service agent pattern at JPMorgan is a direct example — adding a new backend integration means writing a new agent, not modifying the orchestrator.

**24. Tell me about a time an architecture decision you made turned out to be wrong. What did you learn?**
Early in a data platform effort, I underestimated how much schema variability we'd see across enterprise sources, which meant our first ingestion design needed more normalization logic than planned. I learned to prototype against messier real-world data earlier rather than the clean sample sets, and I now budget explicit discovery time before locking an architecture.

**25. How do you approach observability and monitoring for AI systems specifically?**
AI systems need monitoring beyond uptime and latency — you need to track model drift, output quality, and fairness/compliance signals. I designed a patent-pending AI observability platform using lineage and telemetry, so we could trace not just whether a service was healthy, but whether the AI's outputs were trustworthy and explainable.

**26. What's your framework for making architecture decisions under ambiguity?**
Write the decision down with the alternatives considered and the reversibility cost of each — even a lightweight ADR forces rigor. If reversible, bias toward action and iterate; if not, get a second senior opinion before committing.

**27. How do you approach microservices vs. a more monolithic design?**
Team topology should drive service boundaries as much as technical purity — services that always change together shouldn't be split just because "microservices are best practice." Across IBM and OpenText, I split services along domains that different teams owned independently, which is where the real payoff of microservices showed up.

**28. How do you ensure architecture decisions align with business goals?**
I map every major technical initiative back to a business metric before greenlighting it — the campaign automation platform's north star was cycle-time reduction for marketing launches, and every architecture choice got tested against whether it moved that number.

**29. Describe your approach to data governance in a platform you've built.**
At IBM, governance wasn't a bolt-on policy layer — it was built into the lineage and catalog architecture itself, so every dataset carried its provenance and classification as first-class metadata. That's what let us improve traceability by 70% and made downstream compliance and explainability capabilities possible.

**30. How do you balance innovation (e.g., GenAI adoption) with platform stability?**
I isolate experimentation behind clear boundaries — new GenAI capabilities get built and tested in a way that can't destabilize the core platform, then get promoted once they've proven reliable. At OpenText, GenAI-powered retrieval was built as an additive service layer rather than a rework of the core SaaS platform.

