## Section 3: AI, GenAI & Agentic AI Platform Questions (15)

**31. What's your definition of "Agentic AI," and how is it different from a chatbot or a single LLM call?**
Agentic AI involves a system that can plan, take multi-step action, and orchestrate across tools or services toward a goal — not just respond to a single prompt. The JPMorgan platform I built has an Intent agent, an Orchestrator, and domain agents working together to actually execute a workflow (campaign onboarding), not just answer a question about it.

**32. How do you govern an agentic system so it doesn't take unsafe or unauthorized actions?**
Scope each agent's permissions tightly to only what its domain requires, log every action for auditability, and put human-in-the-loop checkpoints at points of real consequence. In a bank, this isn't optional — every automated workflow step needs to be explainable after the fact.

**33. How did you evaluate the ROI of the Agentic AI platform at JPMorgan?**
We measured reduction in manual effort and time-to-launch for marketing campaigns — concrete, business-owned metrics rather than vague "efficiency" claims. That let us make the case for continued investment with numbers stakeholders trusted.

**34. What are the biggest risks with deploying GenAI in production, and how do you mitigate them?**
Hallucination/incorrect output, data leakage, and unpredictable cost at scale are the big three. I mitigate through retrieval-grounding (RAG) to reduce hallucination, strict data boundaries per agent, and cost/latency monitoring built into the observability layer from day one rather than retrofitted.

**35. Tell me about a RAG system you've built or overseen.**
At OpenText, we built GenAI-powered retrieval services as part of the AI SaaS platform, grounding generation in the enterprise's own content rather than the model's parametric knowledge alone, which was critical for accuracy and for customer trust in a SaaS context.

**36. How do you approach prompt engineering as an engineering discipline rather than an art?**
I push for versioned, tested prompts — treating them like code, with regression tests against a fixed eval set so a "small tweak" doesn't silently degrade quality elsewhere. Otherwise prompt changes become an untracked source of production incidents.

**37. How do you think about AI explainability, especially in a regulated industry?**
Explainability has to be designed in, not reconstructed after the fact — I built LLM-powered compliance and explainability capabilities at IBM specifically because regulators and customers needed to understand why the model reached a conclusion, not just what it concluded.

**38. What's your view on the build-vs-integrate decision for LLMs (e.g., fine-tuning vs. prompting vs. using a foundation model API)?**
Default to the least custom option that meets the bar — prompting a strong foundation model first, RAG if you need grounding in proprietary data, and fine-tuning only when you have a well-defined, high-volume task where prompting demonstrably falls short. Fine-tuning has real ongoing maintenance cost that's easy to underestimate.

**39. How do you keep your team's AI/ML skills current given how fast the field moves?**
I lead by example — speaking at PyTorch Conference Europe and the MCP Dev Summit keeps me close to where the field is moving, and I bring that back into internal tech talks and architecture reviews so the team isn't learning about major shifts secondhand.

**40. What's MCP (Model Context Protocol), and why does it matter for enterprise AI platforms?**
MCP standardizes how AI agents connect to external tools and data sources, which matters a lot for enterprise platforms because it reduces the custom integration work needed every time you want an agent to reach a new system. I spoke on this at the MCP Dev Summit Mumbai because it's directly relevant to how we're evolving agent-to-backend integration patterns.

**41. How would you structure a team to build and maintain an AI platform long-term?**
Split ownership between platform/infrastructure (orchestration, observability, governance) and applied/domain teams (specific agents or use cases), with a shared eval and observability layer so quality is measured consistently across every use case rather than reinvented per team.

**42. How do you handle AI model or output quality regressions in production?**
Continuous evaluation against a golden dataset, alerting on drift, and a clear rollback path to a previous prompt/model version — the same rigor as any other production dependency, because an AI regression is often silent (wrong-but-plausible output) rather than a loud outage.

