## Section 6: Behavioral / Situational — STAR-Based (15)

**68. Tell me about your biggest professional achievement.**
Leading the enterprise Agentic AI platform at JPMorgan from architecture through delivery — it changed how marketing campaigns get onboarded from a manual process into a governed, prompt-driven workflow, with a real measurable reduction in launch time. It combined everything I care about: technical depth, business impact, and building something that scales beyond me.

**69. Tell me about a time you failed. What did you learn?**
Early in the IBM data lineage effort, I initially underestimated the schema diversity across enterprise sources, which meant our first ingestion design needed rework. I learned to invest more upfront discovery time on messy real data before locking architecture, and I now build that into how I scope any new data-heavy project.

**70. Describe a time you had to make a decision with incomplete information.**
Deciding to introduce on-device inference (ExecuTorch) at JPMorgan meant committing to an approach before we had full production-scale latency data. I made the call based on early prototyping results and the clear directional benefit to privacy and compliance, with a plan to validate and adjust once we had more data — which we did.

**71. Tell me about a time you had to influence without authority.**
Getting compliance and legal stakeholders comfortable with an AI-driven workflow at JPMorgan required influence, not authority — I don't own their function. I built trust by proactively designing in the audit and governance controls they'd need, rather than waiting for them to raise objections.

**72. Describe a time you took a calculated risk.**
Betting on a chain-of-agents architecture rather than a simpler single-model approach for the campaign automation platform was a real architectural risk — more complexity upfront, for more governability and extensibility later. It paid off because it let us add new domain agents without re-architecting.

**73. Tell me about a time you had to say no to a request from leadership.**
When a compressed timeline would have meant skipping governance controls on the AI platform, I pushed back — not on the goal, but on the shortcut — and proposed a phased rollout that hit the important early milestones without compromising the audit trail a regulated system needs.

**74. Describe a time you drove a significant process improvement.**
Introducing modern CI/CD, observability, and DevOps practices at OpenText wasn't glamorous, but it directly improved delivery speed and reliability for the whole team — sometimes the highest-leverage engineering management work is unglamorous infrastructure, not a flashy feature.

**75. Tell me about a time you had to learn a new technology quickly.**
Bringing Mistral LLM into IBM's compliance and explainability work required getting hands-on with LLM behavior quickly, well before it was "common knowledge" the way it is today. I built a small proof-of-concept myself before asking the team to invest, so I could speak credibly to its tradeoffs.

**76. Describe a time you had to manage up.**
Communicating fraud-detection architecture risk to leadership at JPMorgan meant translating technical latency/accuracy tradeoffs into business risk language they could act on — framing it as "this tradeoff affects false-decline rate and customer experience," not "here's our model architecture."

**77. Tell me about a time your team missed a deadline. How did you handle it?**
I own it with the team rather than assigning individual blame externally, communicate the revised timeline honestly and early rather than at the last minute, and run an internal retro on what would need to change next time — usually it's a scoping or dependency issue, not an effort issue.

**78. Describe a time you had to advocate for a technical investment that wasn't immediately visible to the business.**
The AI observability platform (patent-pending) wasn't a customer-facing feature, so it needed a different pitch — I framed it around risk reduction and faster incident diagnosis, quantifying what an undetected model quality issue could cost, which made the investment case concrete rather than abstract.

**79. Tell me about a time you had to rebuild trust with a team or stakeholder after something went wrong.**
After a scope miss, I found that over-communicating progress in the following phase — smaller, more frequent, verifiable checkpoints — rebuilt confidence faster than any single reassurance conversation could. Trust comes back through demonstrated reliability, not promises.

**80. Describe a time you mentored someone into a bigger role.**
At IBM and OpenText, I made a point of giving senior engineers real ownership of subsystems — not just tasks — as the path to growing them into leads. Watching someone I mentored take ownership of a domain agent end-to-end at JPMorgan, from design through production, was one of the more satisfying outcomes of that platform.

**81. Tell me about a time you had to pivot a technical strategy mid-project.**
When our GenAI retrieval approach at OpenText needed to shift to better ground outputs in customer content, I made the call to invest in RAG rather than push forward with pure prompting — a real strategy pivot mid-build, justified by output quality data, not just intuition.

**82. Describe the most difficult technical decision you've had to make.**
Choosing the chain-of-agents split (Intent/Orchestrator/domain agents) over a simpler monolithic AI service was difficult because it meant more upfront engineering investment for a payoff (governability, extensibility) that wouldn't be visible until later phases — a hard sell until the platform had to scale to new use cases and it proved out.

