## Section 7: Hiring, Mentoring & Talent Development (10)

**83. What do you look for when hiring a senior engineer?**
Judgment under ambiguity matters more than any specific tech-stack match — I probe for how someone reasons through a tradeoff, not whether they can recite a design pattern. I also look for evidence they've grown other engineers, since senior engineers multiply the team, not just their own output.

**84. How do you structure a technical interview loop for your team?**
A mix of a real-world system design problem relevant to what we actually build (not a generic whiteboard puzzle), a coding exercise that reflects real work, and a values/collaboration conversation — because technical skill without collaboration skill doesn't scale in a team setting.

**85. How do you approach diversity in hiring?**
I make sure the funnel itself is broad — sourcing beyond the usual networks — and that the interview process evaluates capability consistently rather than "culture fit" as a proxy for "like me." A more diverse team has consistently produced better technical debate and fewer blind spots in my experience.

**86. Tell me about how you've grown engineers into management roles.**
I look for engineers who naturally start unblocking others and thinking about team-level outcomes, then give them a small scope — leading a project, then a subsystem — before a formal title change, so the transition is tested and supported rather than a sudden jump.

**87. How do you handle a hiring manager (peer) wanting to poach someone from your team?**
I treat it as a signal to check in on that person's growth and satisfaction rather than blocking the move politically — if it's genuinely a better opportunity for them internally, I'd rather they move within the company and stay engaged than leave entirely.

**88. What's your approach to setting individual growth plans for your reports?**
Growth plans should tie to real upcoming work, not generic skill checklists — I look for the next project that stretches someone in the direction they want to grow, so the plan is lived, not filed away after the review cycle.

**89. How do you evaluate technical leadership potential in an engineer who isn't yet a manager?**
I watch for whether people proactively raise risks before being asked, whether others go to them for design input informally, and whether they can explain a tradeoff to a non-technical stakeholder clearly — those are leading indicators well before a title changes.

**90. How do you handle hiring during a hiring freeze or budget constraint?**
I get ruthless about role prioritization — which single hire most reduces risk or unblocks the roadmap — rather than trying to backfill everything proportionally. I've also used the constraint as a forcing function to invest more in current team members' growth into the gap.

**91. What's your philosophy on internal mobility vs. external hiring for senior roles?**
Internal mobility first when someone has the trajectory and institutional context, because ramp time and cultural risk are both lower — but I don't let internal-first become an excuse to avoid bringing in fresh perspective when the team genuinely needs a new pattern of thinking.

**92. Tell me about a time you had to have a hard conversation with someone about their career trajectory.**
When someone's ambitions didn't match the growth opportunities realistically available on the team, I had the honest conversation rather than letting them find out through a disappointing review cycle — better for them to make an informed choice, even if it meant risk of losing them.

