## Section 8: Vision, Strategy & Questions to Ask the Interviewer (8)

**93. Where do you see AI platform engineering heading over the next 2–3 years?**
Toward more autonomous, multi-agent systems handling genuinely end-to-end workflows — but the organizations that win will be the ones who build governance, observability, and explainability in from the start, not bolted on after an incident. That's the thesis behind the patent-pending observability work I've done and where I'd want to keep pushing.

**94. How would you approach your first 90 days in this Sr. Engineering Manager role?**
Weeks 1–3: listen — understand the team, the architecture, and the real pain points, not just the org chart. Weeks 4–8: identify one or two high-leverage improvements (technical or process) and deliver visible progress on them. Weeks 9–12: bring a longer-term roadmap proposal grounded in what I've learned, not a pre-baked plan imported from my last role.

**95. What would you want to change first if you joined this team?**
I'd hold off committing to specifics until I understand the team's actual constraints — but based on my track record, I'd likely look first at observability and delivery pipeline maturity, since that's consistently been the highest-leverage early investment in the platforms I've led.

**96. How do you define success for yourself as a Sr. Engineering Manager, a year in?**
The team should be shipping meaningfully faster and with fewer surprises than when I joined, at least one or two people should have grown into bigger scope, and there should be a technical roadmap the business genuinely trusts — not one they tolerate.

**97. What's a technical opinion you hold that not everyone on your team agrees with?**
That governance and observability aren't the "boring" 20% of an AI platform — they're as core to the product as the model itself, especially in regulated industries. Some engineers want to move faster by treating them as later work; I've pushed back on that consistently, and I think the JPMorgan and IBM outcomes bear it out.

**98. Why are you looking to move on from your current role, and why this company?**
(Personalize this one — the honest, forward-looking version: focus on what you're moving *toward* — bigger scope, a specific technical challenge, or mission alignment — rather than what you're moving away from.)

**99. What questions do you have for me?**
Good ones to ask: "What does success look like for this role in the first 6 months?" / "How does the engineering org currently balance platform investment against feature delivery?" / "What's the biggest technical or organizational risk you're worried about right now?" / "How is AI governance currently owned across the org?"

**100. Is there anything about your background you'd like to add that we haven't covered?**
I'd highlight the combination that's less common: deep hands-on architecture credibility (agentic systems, real-time fraud detection, data governance at enterprise scale) paired with genuine people leadership across global teams, plus staying externally visible in the field through speaking (PyTorch Conference Europe, MCP Dev Summit) — which keeps my technical judgment current, not just my management technique.

